+# -- patches/0550: the key blocks in one scratch buffer (GLM53_TF_SELECT_SCRATCH) -----------------------------------
+#
+# A key block is [n, npc] int32 with npc the block's complete pools: ~512 B a position of the prompt at 512-row
+# sub-blocks, up to GLM53_TF_SELECT_MB. Allocated per call (0065), its size grows a little every sub-block, so every
+# ~4,096 tokens the caching allocator can no longer place it in the previous block's freed segment, takes a new one
+# and keeps the old one cached: the reserved memory grows quadratically with the prompt (~4-6 GiB at 300k, ~16 GiB
+# bound at 1M: ``memsafe.keys_growth``) until something empties the cache (every CUDA graph capture does). Here the
+# blocks are views of one buffer per (device, stream): ``reserve_scratch`` grows it BEFORE a prefill (the prompt's
+# largest block, ``memsafe.select_peak``, rounded up to 16 MiB) and empties the cache when it replaces a smaller one,
+# so nothing is left behind; a block is ``scratch[:n * npc].view(n, npc)``: the same shape, strides and 512-byte-
+# aligned base as ``torch.empty`` gives, and every element is written by ``_scores_rows`` before ``torch.topk`` /
+# ``_gather_sel`` read it (the grid covers rows row0 .. row0 + n - 1 and pools < npc), so the values, the top-k
+# threshold and the selection are the same bits. While a stream captures a CUDA graph, or when the scratch is too
+# small (a caller that did not reserve), the block is allocated as before (``grow`` also grows it then, without
+# emptying the cache: the trim before the next piece does). Modes: ``grow`` (default), ``max`` (GLM53_TF_SELECT_MB at the
+# first reservation), ``off`` (0065's allocation per block).
